# Post-reload coherence, run by the driver after prober_signal_wait confirms a
# new worker is answering. Proves the reloaded worker is HEALTHY: a correct 200
# on the plain location, no leaked descriptor, and nothing on the error path in
# the window around this request.
#
# STRICT pid oracle (no pid_may_change) is correct: by the time this runs a
# single post-reload worker serves, so both probe snapshots see the same worker
# and "same worker pid" is the stronger, true assertion. pid_may_change relaxes
# the oracle only for a case whose OWN before/after window straddles a reload;
# there is none here (the reload happened outside this prober invocation), so
# the directive would be a vacuous no-op and is deliberately omitted. The
# reconnect-and-reload survival claims are the driver's assertions, not this
# file's.
#
# WHY `delta fds_by_kind.*` SPLIT INSTEAD OF A PLAIN `delta fds == 0`. This
# scenario is the harness's most frequent flake (see driver.sh's settle-loop
# comment): the old worker's parked upstream connection is reaped
# ASYNCHRONOUSLY after the reload, so the fd table can still be draining a
# moment after a stable pid answers two consecutive probes -- a settle proves
# quiescence at time T, this assertion runs at T+n, and no settle variant can
# close that gap (two were tried and both failed). `delta fds == 0` sees the
# aggregate count drop by one and reds on a closing descriptor, not a leaked
# one.
#
# The draining descriptor is specifically the dying worker's parked upstream
# SOCKET (backend.c's memcached keepalive connection) -- never a file, an
# anon (eventfd/timerfd/signalfd) or "other" descriptor, none of which this
# scenario's reload path touches. So the assertion is split by kind
# (ngx_test_probe_fd_kinds(), ngx_test_probe.c): the three kinds that cannot
# legitimately move here stay at the original strict `== 0`, and only the
# socket bucket is relaxed to `<= 0` -- it tolerates the known asynchronous
# DECREASE (a descriptor closing) but still reds hard on any INCREASE, which
# is exactly the shape a real socket leak takes (open one, never close it).
# A leaked socket therefore still fails this rule: `<= 0` rejects a positive
# delta exactly as `== 0` would. This is narrower than tolerating "any
# negative delta on the aggregate `fds`" (rejected option (a) in this
# scenario's history) because it cannot also swallow a leaked file/anon/other
# descriptor riding alongside a legitimate socket close -- those three keep
# the original zero-tolerance oracle.

name    the reloaded worker serves cleanly after the idle-close and reload
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds_by_kind.socket <= 0
delta   fds_by_kind.file == 0
delta   fds_by_kind.anon == 0
delta   fds_by_kind.other == 0
no_error_log  \[error\]
